## GUARD VOCABULARY — verification-criteria

### BLACKLIST (terms that mark a criterion as violating — implementation/mechanism, not observable surface)

| Term | Reason |
|---|---|
| `contentstack.stack` | Traces to `contentstack.Stack(options)` in `src/services/contentstack.ts` — the SDK constructor call that builds the delivery client. A criterion referencing this is checking the internal SDK initialization, not what renders on the page. |
| `delivery_token` | Traces to the `delivery_token` key in the exported `options` object in `src/services/contentstack.ts`. This is an internal config field that feeds the delivery path; it is never observed by a content author. |
| `live-preview configuration parameters` | From statement 1: inspects whether the outgoing request to Contentstack "includes live-preview configuration parameters." This prescribes an internal request-payload structure, not a rendered result. |
| `live-preview block` | From statement 5: inspects whether the Stack configuration "does not include any live-preview block." This checks an internal config object structure, not the user-facing surface. |
| `stack configuration` | From statement 5: references the `options` object passed to `contentstack.Stack()`. A criterion checking what the config "sent by our code" contains is verifying an internal data structure, not rendered output. |
| `preview-related environment variables` | From statements 1 and 5: references env vars (`CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN`, `CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST`) that are internal configuration mechanism. A content author never observes environment variables; they are how preview is wired, not what is seen. |
| `request our code sends` | From statement 1: inspects the outgoing HTTP request payload to Contentstack. This is a white-box check on internal network calls, not a black-box observation of the rendered page. |

### WHITELIST (terms that name the observable surface — never flagged)

| Term | Reason |
|---|---|
| `draft content` | What a content author sees rendered when previewing — the direct observable surface of this ticket. |
| `published content` | What a content author or visitor sees rendered in the default delivery path — the observable baseline. |
| `published version` | The observable counterpart to draft content; describes what the page shows, not how it is fetched. |
| `page renders` | Describes the observable act of content appearing on screen — the black-box surface. |
| `pages render` | Same as above, plural form; describes visible rendering behavior. |
| `error state` | An observable user-facing condition (error message, fallback display) that a tester can see and verify. |
| `preview mode` | The observable user-facing mode the content author enables to see drafts — a testable condition on the surface, not an internal flag. |
| `content author` | The end user this ticket serves; naming the actor is always observable-surface vocabulary. |
| `does not crash` | Observable behavior a tester can verify: the application stays running. |
| `regression` | Describes an observable degradation in existing content delivery — a black-box comparison of before/after rendering. |

### Notes

- `options` was considered as a blacklist candidate (it is the exported config object in `contentstack.ts`) but rejected as too generic — it would fire on unrelated statements and train users to ignore the guard.
- `mocked` / `mock` appear in several statements but describe test infrastructure, not implementation vocabulary; they are neither blacklist nor whitelist.
- `preview query parameter` (statement 3) is borderline: a tester can set it, but it names a mechanism, not an observation. It was not added to the blacklist because statement 3's actual observation ("all pages render published content") is compliant regardless of the setup clause.
- The whitelist wins on any overlap: `preview mode` appears in both compliant and violating statements but always describes the observable user condition, so it is whitelisted.
